<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Hus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John Hus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Hus was a Czech priest and reformer, rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">University of Prague</a>, whose criticism of church abuses drew on the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a>. Summoned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> under a safe-conduct from King Sigismund, he refused to recant and was burned at the stake there in July 1415. His execution provoked the Hussite movement and wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and he was later seen as a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-hus.jpg" alt="Jan Hus" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Murer 1587 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stimmer_Jan_Hus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jan Hus</b>, a Prague preacher influenced by the writings of John Wycliffe, attacked church abuses and was burned at the Council of Constance in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The followers of John Wycliffe in England were driven underground, and <b>Jan Hus</b> was burned as a heretic at the Council of Constance in 1415, although his followers kept a church of their own in Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first of the three Defenestrations of Prague, a crowd of radical Hussites, followers of the Czech reformer <b>Jan Hus</b>, threw members of the city council from a window, killing seven.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Czech reformer <b>Jan Hus</b>, condemned as a heretic by the Council of Constance despite a promise of safe conduct, was burned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerome of Prague, a Czech philosopher and theologian and one of the chief followers of <b>Jan Hus</b>, was burned for heresy at the Council of Constance.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-30">30 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It deposed two, accepted the resignation of the third and elected Martin V, but also condemned <b>Jan Hus</b> to the stake.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-5">5 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
